A1. This sometimes comes for dinner stewed, and often gets in the soup, too. ~ TOMATO
A7. The day Scotchmen stay home. ~ TAG
A10. I find my coffee surrounded by this every morning. ~ CUP
A13. These hang around in the kitchen. ~ AROMAS
A14. Whenever you mention this state to a Spaniard he sees red. ~ COLORADO
A16. A five thousand two hundred and eighty footer. ~ MILER
A17. Frog propeller. ~ HOP
A18. These folks lead a high life, and sing all day. ~ BIRDS
A19. This never gives out at tea time. ~ GAS
A20. You'll get tender results if you treat your chicken this way. ~ ROAST
A22. A source of sweet stuff. ~ BEET
A23. Dumb Dora gets all her new coats from this tree. ~ FIR
A24. If this is too high, you'll never get over it. ~ WALL
A25. A lawyer's regard. ~ RE
A27. How a night watchman's dreams start. ~ DRE
A28. You may be down, but you're never out when you're like this. ~ IN
A29. The old ancestral home. ~ CAVE
A30. This may end in a crash. ~ DASH
A32. The office daddy (abbr.) ~ SR
A33. Get out (Refined landlordese). ~ VACATE
A35. This produces music from seven different places at once. ~ SEPTET
A37. The kind of duck that comes in late. ~ LAME
A38. This'll get any car out of a stall. ~ TOW
A39. Only one out of a hundred. ~ UNIT
A41. This pretty maiden is all wet. ~ NEREID
A43. On a windy day, this rivals the ocean for waves. ~ BANNER
A45. The way to go from New York to Texas (abbr.) ~ SW
A47. A good year for old hens. ~ LEAP
A48. A subtle offspring (you'll never see this one). ~ AURA
A49. "And I don't mean maybe!" ~ NO
A50. A good place to rest before a drive. ~ TEE
A52. The bird with a big bill. (abbr.) ~ DR
A53. Your best customer in war. ~ ALLY
A54. Earl Carroll's girls wear less clothes than this. ~ FEW
A55. Belonging to the King. ~ REXS
A57. A man of much wealth (until the tax collectors find him). ~ NABOB
A59. Look close; this isn't very plain. ~ DIM
A60. You just know this one is foolish. ~ IDIOT
A62. The first of reenforcements. ~ REE
A63. Got new razor blades in Scotland. ~ HONED
A65. Can you see through this? It's perfectly clear. ~ PELLUCID
A67. Commit again (not a sin, but a sinner). ~ REMAND
A68. A trek that stopped just before the end. ~ TRE
A69. Make the best of this; it's not good. ~ BAD
A70. Small fry. ~ SMELTS

D1. This is the Dobbs in Scotland. ~ TAM
D2. This always comes first. ~ ORIGINAL
D3. It's a tough grind for this sometimes. ~ MOLAR
D4. That city in Iowa. ~ AMES
D5. Soft feather bed. ~ TAR
D6. You can't pick a bone with me over this, though it is a good opening. ~ OS
D7. You can take these for a spin. ~ TOPS
D8. Little Alfred. ~ AL
D9. This is in the Navy now ~ GOB
D10. What the Reno-bound lady said her husband never did for her. ~ CARED
D11. Milk routes. ~ UDDERS
D12. This will make you take notice. ~ POSTER
D14. Makings of a hot time. ~ COAL
D15. A woman maker. ~ RIB
D17. Nothing with something around it. ~ HOLE
D20. A woman's fur coat bill will make her husband do this. ~ RAVE
D21. Skip this—you're liable to get caught. ~ TRAP
D23. I tried for five, but couldn't make it. ~ FIV
D24. What the flowers were when it rained. ~ WATERED
D26. This may be found where tide and current meet (and it's a mouthful). ~ ESTUARY
D29. People who ride this ship usually have lots of sand. ~ CAMEL
D30. This comes out of the ground and lies on the grass until the sun comes out. ~ DEW
D31. When my girl uses this, I see red. ~ HENNA
D34. The newlyweds' diner. ~ CAN
D35. When you're under this, your worries should end. ~ SOD
D36. Burglars should tread lightly on this kind of roof. ~ TIN
D38. What good waiters wait for. ~ TIP
D40. This is where Alfred E. Smith got his start. ~ TENEMENT
D42. Acquire by labor. ~ EARN
D43. What a fat man uses to get light. ~ BULB
D44. If you go for this with another woman, wifie may start one. ~ ROW
D45. Some people get this way from playing strip poker (var.). ~ STRIPT
D46. What the gardener uses on the things he didn't plant. ~ WEEDER
D48. Fragrant resin or wood of the agalloch. ~ ALOE
D51. A man with a home he can't go to. ~ EXILE
D53. If it is necessary to lie, this is a good place to do it. ~ ABED
D54. What a woman's last word is. ~ FINAL
D56. He may make it hot for you, but he's a good friend. ~ SOL
D58. This is pretty dry. ~ ARID
D59. What the man in the Brown Derby wears it for. ~ DOME
D61. This will help you live a clean life. ~ TUB
D63. This just hangs around the edge. ~ HEM
D64. An obliging man when you need someone to pull for you (abbr.). ~ DDS
D66. Half cash. ~ CA
D67. A good finish for beers. ~ RS


